+    "## Normal Random Variables\n\n### Finding the Expected Value\n\nThe expected value of a standard normal distribution with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$ is just $\\mu$. For other normal distributions, the expected value can be calculated using moment generating functions (MGFs). The following is the MGF of a normally distributed random variable $X$ ~ $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\phi_X(t) = e^{\\mu t + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 t^2}\n\\label{eq:9} \\tag{9}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nIn general, the $n$th moment of a random variable $X$ is equal to $E[X^n]$, which is found by differentiating the MGF $n$ times and evaluating at $t = 0$:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\nE[X^n] = \\phi_X^{(n)}(0), \\quad \\text{so } E[X] = \\phi_X'(0) = \\mu\n\\label{eq:10} \\tag{10}\n\\end{equation*}\n\n#### Example\n\n*If $\\text{log}(X)$ ~ $N(0,1)$, what's the expected value of $X$?* \n\nSince $X = e^{\\text{log}(X)}$, we can evaluate the MGF of $\\text{log}(X)$ at $t = 1$: $E[X] = E[e^{\\text{log}(X)}] = \\phi_{\\text{log}(X)}(1) = e^{(0) + \\frac{1}{2}(1)^2} = e^{\\frac{1}{2}}$. (Note that $e^{\\mu + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2}$ is the mean of the *lognormal* distribution--i.e. $E[e^Y]$ for $Y$ ~ $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$--not the mean of $Y$ itself.)\n\n### Finding the Sum\n\nThe sum of two independent and identically distributed (IID) normal random variables $X$ ~ $N(\\mu_x, \\sigma_x^2)$ and $Y$ ~ $N(\\mu_y, \\sigma_y^2)$ is also normal, i.e.  $(X + Y)$ ~ $N(\\mu_x + \\mu_y, \\sigma_x^2 + \\sigma_y^2)$. Similarly, this applies to the sum of any number of independent normal random variables. \n\n#### Proof using convolutions\n\nThe PDFs of independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\nf_X(x) &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi} \\sigma_x} {e}^{-\\frac{(x - \\mu_x)^{2}}{2 {\\sigma_x^2}}} \\\\\nf_Y(y) &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi} \\sigma_y} {e}^{-\\frac{(y - \\mu_y)^{2}}{2 {\\sigma_y^2}}}\n\\end{split}\n\\label{eq:11} \\tag{11}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nAnd the distribution of their sum $Z$ is the convolution of $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\nf_Z(z) &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f_X(x) f_Y(z-x) dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2 \\pi \\sigma_x \\sigma_y} {e}^{-\\frac{\\sigma_x^2(z - x - \\mu_y)^2 + \\sigma_y^2(x - \\mu_x)^2}{2\\sigma_x^2 \\sigma_y^2}} dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2 \\pi \\sigma_x \\sigma_y} {e}^{-\\frac{x^2(\\sigma_x^2 + \\sigma_y^2) - 2x(\\sigma_x^2 (z - \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2 \\mu_x) + \\sigma_x^2 (z^2 +\\mu_y^2 -2z \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2 \\mu_x^2}{2\\sigma_x^2 \\sigma_y^2}} dx\n\\end{split}\n\\label{eq:12} \\tag{12}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nDefining $\\sigma_z^2 = \\sigma_x^2 + \\sigma_y^2$ and completing the square gives us:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\nf_Z(z) &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{\\frac{2 \\pi \\sigma_z \\sigma_x \\sigma_y}{\\sigma_z}} {e}^{-\\frac{x^2 - \\frac{2x(\\sigma_x^2 (z - \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2 \\mu_x) + \\sigma_x^2 (z^2 +\\mu_y^2 -2z \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2 \\mu_x^2}{\\sigma_z^2}}{2\\biggl(\\frac{\\sigma_x \\sigma_y}{\\sigma_z}\\biggr)^2}} dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{\\frac{2 \\pi \\sigma_z \\sigma_x \\sigma_y}{\\sigma_z}} {e}^{-\\frac{\\biggl(x - \\frac{\\sigma_x^2 (z - \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2\\mu_x}{\\sigma_z^2} \\biggr)^2 - \\biggl(\\frac{\\sigma_x^2 (z - \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2\\mu_x}{\\sigma_z^2} \\biggr)^2 + \\frac{\\sigma_x^2 (z - \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2\\mu_x}{\\sigma_z^2}}{2\\biggl(\\frac{\\sigma_x \\sigma_y}{\\sigma_z}\\biggr)^2}} dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi} \\sigma_z} {e}^{-\\frac{(z - (\\mu_x + \\mu_y))^2}{2\\sigma_z^2}} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi \\sigma_x \\sigma_y}{\\sigma_z}} {e}^{-\\frac{\\biggl(x - \\frac{\\sigma_x^2 (z - \\mu_y) + \\sigma_y^2\\mu_x}{\\sigma_z^2} \\biggr)^2}{2\\biggl(\\frac{\\sigma_x \\sigma_y}{\\sigma_z}\\biggr)^2}} dx\n\\end{split}\n\\label{eq:13} \\tag{13}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nThe integral is a normal density distribution on $x$ with a standard deviation of $\\frac{\\sigma_x \\sigma_y}{\\sigma_z}$, which we know is equal to 1. Therefore, the PDF of $X + Y$ is the following normal distribution with a mean of $\\mu_x + \\mu_y$ and standard deviation $\\sigma_z^2$: \n\n\\begin{equation*}\nf_Z(z) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi} \\sigma_z} {e}^{-\\frac{(z - (\\mu_x + \\mu_y))^2}{2\\sigma_z^2}}\n\\label{eq:14} \\tag{14}\n\\end{equation*}\n\n#### Proof using MGF\n\nMGFs can be used to uniquely determine the distribution sum of random variables. The MGFs of normally distributed random variables $X$ and $Y$ are:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\phi_X(t) &= e^{\\mu_x t + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma_x^2 t^2} \\\\\n\\phi_Y(t) &= e^{\\mu_y t + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma_y^2 t^2}\n\\end{split}\n\\label{eq:15} \\tag{15}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nThe MGF of $X+Y$ is simply the product of the two independent random variables $X$ and $Y$:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\phi_{X+Y}(t) &= \\phi_X(t) \\cdot \\phi_Y(t) \\\\\n&= e^{\\mu_x t + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma_x^2 t^2} \\cdot e^{\\mu_y t + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma_y^2 t^2} \\\\\n&= e^{(\\mu_x + \\mu_y)t + \\frac{1}{2}(\\sigma_x^2 + \\sigma_y^2)t^2}\n\\end{split}\n\\label{eq:16} \\tag{16}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nWhich proves that $X + Y$ ~ $N(\\mu_x + \\mu_y, \\sigma_x^2 + \\sigma_y^2)$. See [here](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moment-generating_function#Examples) for MGFs of other distributions. \n\n### Finding the Product\n\n(Using MGFs) The product of two normal random variables is **not** normal. For concreteness, consider the special case where $X$ and $Y$ are IID *standard* normal, $X, Y$ ~ $N(0, 1)$ (not the general $X, Y$ defined above). Then $XY$ can be written as the difference of two scaled Chi-squared random variables:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\nXY = \\left( \\frac{X + Y}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left( \\frac{X - Y}{2} \\right)^2\n\\label{eq:17} \\tag{17}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent standard normals, $\\frac{X + Y}{2}$ ~ $N(0, \\frac{1}{2})$ and $\\frac{X - Y}{2}$ ~ $N(0, \\frac{1}{2})$. Recall that if $Z$ ~ $N(0, 1)$, then $Z^2$ ~ $\\chi_1^2$. So $\\left( \\frac{X + Y}{2} \\right)^2$ is a Chi-squared random variable with 1 degree of freedom scaled by $\\frac{1}{2}$. (A Chi-squared distribution with $n$ degrees of freedom has variance $\\text{var} = 2n$ and MGF $\\phi(t) = (1-2t)^{-\\frac{n}{2}}$.) The scaled terms have MGFs:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\phi_{\\left( \\frac{X + Y}{2} \\right)^2} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-t}} \\\\\n\\phi_{-\\left( \\frac{X - Y}{2} \\right)^2} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+t}}\n\\end{split}\n\\label{eq:18} \\tag{18}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nwhere $-\\left( \\frac{X - Y}{2} \\right)^2$ is a *negated* scaled Chi-squared random variable (a negated Chi-squared random variable is not itself Chi-squared). The two terms are independent, so the MGF of $XY$ is their product:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\phi_{XY} &= \\phi_{\\left( \\frac{X + Y}{2} \\right)^2} \\cdot \\phi_{-\\left( \\frac{X - Y}{2} \\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-t}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+t}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-t^2}}\n\\end{split}\n\\label{eq:19} \\tag{19}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-t^2}}$ is not of the form $(1-2t)^{-\\frac{n}{2}}$, $XY$ does **not** follow a Chi-squared distribution--the product of independent normals has its own distribution with no closed-form elementary density. Note this argument used that $X$ and $Y$ are independent *standard* normals, so it doesn't extend to general $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ variables or to products of more than two normals. However, the product of the PDFs for $X$ and $Y$ *is* a bivariate normal distribution with zero correlation. "
    ]
