+    "## Variance, Covariance, and Correlation\n\nThe **variance** of a random variable $X$ can be written in the terms of the standard deviation of $X$, $\\sigma_X$, and the expected value of X:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\text{var}(X) &= \\sigma_X^2 \\\\\n&= E[(X - E[X])^2] \\\\\n&= E[X^2] - E[X]^2\n\\end{split}\n\\tag{7}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nThe **covariance** of random variables $X$ and $Y$, each with $n$ values (i.e. $x_1, x_2, \\cdots, x_n$ and $y_1, y_2, \\cdots, y_n$), is:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\text{Cov}(X, Y) &= E[XY] - E[X]E[Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i-\\bar{x})(y_i-\\bar{y})\n\\end{split}\n\\tag{8}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nwhere $\\bar{x}$ and $\\bar{y}$ are the means of $X$ and $Y$, respectively. Covariance matrices show the covariance between variables. They are symmetrical and positive semidefinite--meaning all of their eigenvalues are greater than or equal to zero (individual entries can still be negative). The determinant test is used to determine whether a matrix is a covariance matrix by computing the determinants of the growing submatrices in the upper left corner of the matrix. If they are all positive semidefinite, then the matrix is a covariance matrix.\n\nAnd the **Pearson correlation coefficient** of $X$ and $Y$ is:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\rho(X, Y) &= \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X, Y)}{\\sqrt{\\text{var}(X)\\text{var}(Y)}} \\\\\n&= \\beta \\frac{\\sigma_x}{\\sigma_y}\n\\end{split}\n\\tag{9}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nwhere $\\beta$ is the slope of $Y = \\alpha + \\beta X$. If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\text{Cov}(X, Y) = 0$ and $\\rho(X, Y) = 0$. For the geometric derivation of the correlation, see [here](https://medium.com/@ns2586/geometric-interpretation-of-the-correlation-between-two-variables-4011fb3ea18e).\n\n### Examples\n\n#### Example 1\n\n*(From [HackerRank](https://www.hackerrank.com/challenges/s10-mcq-7/problem)) The regression line of $y$ on $x$ is $3x + 4y + 8 = 0$ and $4x + 3y + 7 = 0$ for $x$ on $y$. Find $\\rho$.*\n\nFrom the two regression lines, we get:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n3x + 4y + 8 = 0 &\\Rightarrow y = -2 - \\frac{3}{4}x \\\\\n4x + 3y + 7 = 0 &\\Rightarrow x = -\\frac{7}{4} - \\frac{3}{4}y\n\\end{split}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nMultiplying $\\rho$ of both regression lines gives us:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n\\rho^2 &= \\left( -\\frac{3}{4} \\frac{\\sigma_x}{\\sigma_y} \\right) \\left( -\\frac{3}{4} \\frac{\\sigma_y}{\\sigma_x} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{16}\n\\end{split}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nSince the slopes of the two regression lines are both negative, we know that $x$ and $y$ are negatively correlated. Therefore, $\\rho = -\\frac{3}{4}$.\n\n#### Example 2\n\n*Given $\\rho(X, Y) = 0.9$ and $\\rho(Y, Z) = 0.8$, find $\\rho(X, Z)$.*\n\nWe don't know much about $X$, $Y$, or $Z$, so we have to express $\\rho(X, Z)$ as a range of values. Let $\\rho(X, Z) = a$. Then, we have the following correlation matrix for $XYZ$:\n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n1 & 0.9 & a \\\\\n0.9 & 1 & 0.8 \\\\\na & 0.8 & 1\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nWe can solve for $a$ by finding the determinant of the correlation matrix above.  \n\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\begin{split}\n&\\left| \\begin{matrix}\n1 & 0.9 & a\\\\0.9 & 1 & 0.8\\\\a & 0.8 & 1\n\\end{matrix} \n\\right| \\\\\n&= (1)\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n1&0.8\\\\\n0.8&1\n\\end{bmatrix} \n- (0.9) \n\\begin{bmatrix}\n0.9&0.8\\\\\na&1\n\\end{bmatrix} \n+ (a) \n\\begin{bmatrix}\n0.9&1\\\\\na&0.8\n\\end{bmatrix} \n\\geq 0 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow -a^2 + 1.44a - 0.45 \\geq 0 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow 0.4585 \\leq a \\leq 0.9815\n\\end{split}\n\\end{equation*}\n\nTherefore, $0.4585 \\leq a \\leq 0.9815$.\n\n#### Example 3\n\n*For three assets $A$, $B$, and $C$, the correlation coefficient of $A$ and $B$ is 0.9 and is 0.8 for $B$ and $C$. Can $A$ and $C$ have a correlation coefficient of 0.1?*\n\n$$\n\\text{ABC} = \\begin{bmatrix}\nV_{A,A} & C_{A,B} & C_{A,C} \\\\\nC_{B,A} & V_{B,B} & C_{B,C} \\\\\nC_{C,A} & C_{C,B} & V_{C,C}\n\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\n1 & 0.9 & 0.1 \\\\\n0.9 & 1 & 0.8 \\\\\n0.1 & 0.8 & 1\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo solve this, we'll need to use the determinant test:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n|1| &= 1 \\\\\n\\left| \\begin{matrix}\n1 & 0.9 \\\\\n0.9 & 1\n\\end{matrix} \\right| &= 0.19 \\\\\n|\\text{ABC}| &= (1)\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n1 & 0.8 \\\\\n0.8 & 1\n\\end{bmatrix} \n- (0.9) \\begin{bmatrix}\n0.9 & 0.8 \\\\\n0.1 & 1\n\\end{bmatrix} \n+ (0.1) \\begin{bmatrix}\n0.9 & 0.1 \\\\\n0.1 & 0.8\n\\end{bmatrix} = -0.316\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $\\text{ABC}$ is not positive, so it can't be a positive semidefinite matrix. Therefore, $\\text{ABC}$ cannot be a covariance matrix if the correlation coefficient between $A$ and $C$ is 0.1."
    ]
